can democracy still deliver president  biden is determined to show the world  that it can and will the white house  today opened a virtual gathering with  representatives from over a hundred  countries the two-day summit for  democracy is part of an effort to  counter authoritarian regimes but the  guest list is raising lots of questions  and the summit also comes at a fragile  time for america's own democracy as we  approach the one-year anniversary of the  siege of the u.s capitol so let's  discuss all of this with michael brown  with he is president of freedom house  it's a non-profit that conducts research  and advocacy on political freedom and  human rights michael welcome to quick  take before we get to who's attending  who's not and the timing  what does biden want to accomplish with  this summit what would success look like  for him  well i think the president has properly  identified  the rise of authoritarianism and the  kind of corresponding decline of  democracy which we at freedom house have  chronicled over the past 15 years  is a very serious problem for the world  uh this is uh  they're not presidents who have really  tried to put the focus on this issue the  way he has he said during the campaign  that he hoped that he would hold a  summit and he's and he's doing it so i  think what he hopes to gain from this is  to try to  build solidarity and greater attention  from the world's democracies to do  better to function more effectively and  to be better prepared to counter the  authoritarian threat  michael what was your reaction to the  guest list was there anyone included who  you found to be a little bit surprising  and was there anyone excluded that you  thought was interesting  well i certainly think the guest list  was kind of a  function if you will of a little bit of  geopolitics i think most of the  countries uh that are at the summit you  know we have no problems with there are  they are they're democracies you know  some are stronger democracies than  others you know there are a few  countries that have been invited that uh  are you know say a little bit weird uh  you know one that comes to mind would be  pakistan uh which has not been uh  performing strongly as far as democracy  is concerned in our ratings but once you  invite india to the summit then you then  the united states couldn't get away with  not inviting uh pakistan so i think  certainly there are geopolitics involved  i think the important thing  to really focus on though is what we're  trying to get out of the summit and the  point is that every country that has  come to the summit has has to make some  kind of commitment  of how they are going to prove how  they're going to improve their  own democracy how they're going to  support  uh the growth of democracy whether it's  supporting independent journalism  fighting corruption there are a lot of  things that people can do and so i think  the test for whether this summit is a  success will come over the next couple  of years when we see whether the  countries actually deliver on what they  promised to do today and then and in the  next few months gotcha no instant  results in other words so one  country or one government that's been  invited is taiwan which officially is  not even a country right um this has  caused a lot of anger on beijing's part  because beijing still considers taiwan  to be part of china what is the u.s  hoping to accomplish here what message  is it trying to send and what can taiwan  really  offer and contribute to this summit  well one thing that's really amazing  about taiwan is that in a world in which  we have  a lot of backsliding democracies a lot  of  violations of human rights taiwan is  actually a a kind of a success story  30 to 40 years ago it was essentially a  military dictatorship  today  for a lot of different reasons a lot of  hard work on the part of the taiwanese  people it is one of the most thriving  democracies in the world and i would say  it also has kind of given a lie to the  idea that the chinese people are not  interested in democracy because uh it's  the chinese people who  who inhabit taiwan  so i think china the mainland china is a  bit threatened by this maybe a lot  threatened by this uh and they're not  happy  that taiwan is there but i think it was  a good call by the administration  because we really believe at freedom  house that that taiwan uh is really a  strong defender of human rights  democracy freedom and it's good to see  them there  michael i want to get your take on the  administration's kind of  defense of of democracy within the u.s  right i mean we're approaching the  one-year anniversary of the capital  insurrection  former president trump calling the  election results into question even  though we know that's not uh that's not  true but you know how does biden defend  the state of democracy in the u.s during  this summit  well first of all we have to be honest  that u.s democracy has not been  functioning as well as we would hope it  would be functioning uh in the in recent  years  at freedom house  we put out an annual survey of kind of  the health of democracy every year and  just as there's been an erosion of  democracy around the world we see over  the last 10 years there's been an  erosion of democracy in the united  states now we have to have some context  for that  the fact is the united states is still  a free country  a lot of great elements of its democracy  uh i'm a former reporter like you uh  it's a it's a great country for  safeguarding  journalists for having strong legal  protections for journalism which is a  cornerstone of democracy uh but but  we've had trouble and and i do think  that the bible administration has not  shied away from that it strikes me that  in the comments that i've seen from the  president the secretary of state and  others there has been a recognition  that uh the united states also has to  try to improve its own democracy  not easy there's such polarization it's  very difficult to get things through the  u.s congress but i do think that the  great thing about america is that uh we  there's a capacity for renewal and so i  think uh it's appropriate for us to have  the summit and it's appropriate for the  president and others to acknowledge some  of the shortcomings in u.s democracy the  shortcomings and perhaps the  contradictions as well because another  test of u.s democracy is how a lot of  republican-led states are  passing voting restrictions one year  before the midterms even as certain  places like new york city are looking to  let non-citizens vote in local elections  as well the definition of democracy and  who gets to participate in it changes  according to where you are and each side  can claim that they're doing the right  thing  correct we're we're a federal system  it's up to the states uh how they're  going to run elections uh there are 50  they're 50 different rules for how  that's going to happen and there's no  question  that the uh something that is kind of  sacrosanct to democracy which is the  right to vote has become deeply  politicized i mean we think that uh it  should be  uh it's an honor to be able to vote and  we think it you know there should be as  few restrictions as possible uh we  recognize that sometimes states have to  take steps to make sure that that that  that  that votes that there's integrity to the  voting but  as far as i can see there's not been  really significant  fraud in u.s elections over the last  number of years in fact most independent  studies say that u.s elections certainly  the one in 2020 was well run so it's  disappointing to see efforts to uh to  restrict voting well you know michael  it's interesting we haven't even had  time to touch on russia in this  discussion which is obviously a huge uh  issue right now as well but i'm curious  in your view to what extent do summits  like this have an impact on on global  freedom uh and really move the needle  here  i think they can have a potential impact  i think we have to be a little bit uh  uh honest that uh there's a lot of  factors that go into the health of  democracy and just having a two-day  summit it's nice  i do think it's very significant that  the united states president is devoting  two whole days of his schedule to try to  you know  draw attention to the challenges facing  democracy i think this is one of the  biggest issues in the world today up  there with global warming and uh and i  think it's very significant that the  united states is having this summit is  trying to you know focus attention on  this very serious problem  now we have to deliver on that or the  u.s government and the other democracies  that have come to the summit have to  deliver and try to and try to be more  effective in in protecting democracy if  we're if we're on this show a year or  two from now and there's been not much  progress i worry that it will deepen  cynicism so you know the president has  properly called the summit but he also  we also now need to deliver all right  well michael we'll have to follow up  with you a year or two from today  michael really appreciate your time with  us that was michael abramowicz president  of freedom house
 

